{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"GUARD LANDED in commit 2f2a981c61 (not pushed). Plus the measurement AC1 asked for, and a\nSECOND poisoner that is live today.\n\n## AC1 ANSWERED BY INSTRUMENTATION: 0 of the 5 negative assertions are void\n\nA temporary pytest plugin recorded, per test, `logging.getLogger(\"rebar\").propagate` and the\ncaptured-record count at the end of the call phase, over a full default-selection run\n(`-n 6 --dist worksteal`). All five targets were reached and ALL FIVE had propagate=True:\n\n  tests/unit/test_agent_build_invariants.py::test_usage_plausibility_silent_on_normal\n  tests/unit/test_usage_log.py::test_summarize_marks_unknown_model_unpriced\n  tests/unit/test_config_default_assignee.py::test_key_is_known_no_unknown_warning\n  tests/unit/test_config_llm.py::test_core_load_config_does_not_warn_on_llm_table\n  tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_check_canonical_heldout.py::test_no_spurious_truncation_warning\n\nThe plugin also emitted a row for ANY test observed with propagate=False. There were none.\n\nLiveness confirmed positively, not just by absence: injecting the exact record each assertion\nforbids (matching logger + token) makes ALL FIVE go red - `5 failed in 0.82s`. So none is a\ngreen no-op; nothing had to be restored or deleted under AC4.\n\nWhy they are immune to the level vector below: each uses `caplog.at_level(WARNING, logger=...)`\nor asserts at WARNING, and a logger level only gates at the ORIGINATING logger, so a raised\n`rebar` level does not reach them. The victims of the level vector are INFO-level assertions.\n\n## WHY THE PROPAGATE VECTOR FOUND NOTHING - pytest already half-mitigates it\n\n`_pytest.logging.catching_logs.__enter__` (pytest 9.1.1) attaches the capture handler to every\nlogger that is ALREADY non-propagating. Its own comment names the hole: \"will miss loggers that\n*become* non-propagating after the `__enter__`\". So the void window is one test PHASE wide, not\nprocess-wide. The description premise that one call voids everything downstream does not hold on\nthis pytest; the ticket comment 2 prediction that the 5 would show as vacuous is therefore not\nwhat the instrumentation found. The window is still silent, and relying on a _pytest internal is\nnot a control, so the guard still earns its place.\n\n## SECOND POISONER, PROVEN, AND IT IS THE LIVE ONE: the LEVEL, not propagation\n\n`rebar._logging.install_stderr_handler()` does `logger.setLevel(WARNING)` on the shared `rebar`\nlogger, process-global, never restored. `rebar._cli.main()` calls it on EVERY invocation, and 11\ntest files call `main()` in-process. After that, an INFO record on any `rebar.*` logger is dropped\nAT THE ORIGINATING LOGGER, and `caplog.at_level(logging.INFO)` with no `logger=` argument CANNOT\nrescue it because it raises the ROOT level only.\n\nThree-test probe, clean tree - identical assertion either side of a `main([\"--help\"])` call:\n`test_a_baseline` PASSES, `test_b_poison_via_cli_main` prints `AFTER level: 30`,\n`test_c_same_assertion_after` FAILS. This is the reported b718 symptom exactly, and unlike the\npropagate vector it fires in every default run.\n\nDETERMINISTIC REPRODUCTION OF THE REPORTED FLAKE, in-repo, one process:\n  pytest -p no:randomly tests/unit/test_llm_setup_cli.py tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py\n  with the guard      -> 17 passed\n  guard mutated off   -> 1 failed: test_success_telemetry_survives_an_empty_usage_dict\n                         - StopIteration at test_agent_call_seam.py:159\nByte-identical to the originally reported failure. The intermittency was never mysterious: it was\nwhether worksteal put a CLI-invoking test on the same worker before this one. The guard both makes\nit deterministic and fixes it.\n\n## THE GUARD, AND WHY THE TWO VECTORS ARE TREATED DIFFERENTLY\n\nAutouse fixture in tests/conftest.py, detection in tests/_log_integrity.py (same split as the\nrepo-isolation guard, so the self-test drives the same code, not a copy):\n\n  * propagate=False -> FAIL the test, named at the SOURCE (\"<nodeid> disabled it during its own\n    body\"), and restore propagation so no downstream victim is blamed instead. A separate\n    setup-phase message covers an out-of-band (import/collection-time) kill and explicitly does\n    NOT accuse the test it lands on.\n  * level raised -> CONTAIN: snapshot at setup, restore at teardown, no blame. Raising it is a\n    legitimate side effect of exercising a real entrypoint in-process. Containment is strictly\n    stronger than blame here for coverage integrity - every test then starts from the same level,\n    so its log assertions hold regardless of run order - whereas failing would force ~11 files to\n    add cleanup for no additional safety.\n\n## MUTATION EVIDENCE BOTH DIRECTIONS (permanent, in the self-test)\n\ntests/unit/test_caplog_coverage_integrity.py, 11 tests, via pytester so the same poisoned file is\nrun with and without the guard - the guard is the only difference:\n\n  propagate vector: unguarded -> 1 passed, ret 0 (the fail-open reproduced: a vacuous assertion\n                    reports success); guarded -> 1 passed + 1 error, ret != 0, and the output\n                    names test_kills_propagation_mid_capture as the source.\n  level vector:     unguarded -> a later INFO assertion FAILS; guarded -> 2 passed.\n  no cascade:       the test after the poisoner sees propagate=True and captures normally.\n  clean run:        unaffected, ret 0.\n\n## COORDINATION WITH b718 - no duplicated mechanism\n\nb718 (commit 590e0e070f) is a PRODUCTION-code fix: configure_logging() stops mutating propagate.\n9ac2 is a TEST-HARNESS guard covering the class. Neither reimplements the other: b718 removes one\nsource, the guard catches any future source plus the level vector b718 does not touch. b718 must\nland first, or the guard fails test_configure_logging_emits_rebar_info_to_stdout at teardown -\nwhich is itself the guard demonstrating it catches the source.\n\nGates by exit code: make lint 0, make typecheck 0. Not pushed to Gerrit; ticket left in_progress."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785727683958656001,"uuid":"3be3c852-86f8-45ec-ab3f-cd1dd7af9dd6"}